WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.860
- What's up, guys, Dr. Mike here.

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:03.427
People often ask me,

00:00:03.427 --> 00:00:06.170
"What's it really like to be a doctor?"

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:08.600
- Hello, sick people,
and their loved ones.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:09.504
- Come on.

00:00:09.504 --> 00:00:11.760
(gasping)
(beeping)

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.048
You gotta be kidding me.

00:00:13.048 --> 00:00:15.060
(dramatic music)
(yelling)

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.430
All right, all right,
enough, enough, enough.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:18.290
Let's talk about what it's really like

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:19.400
to be in the hospital.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.340
I've been at numerous hospitals,

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.560
I've spent countless hours in hospitals,

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.750
so I want to share with you my journey

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.850
and what it's like behind the scenes,

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.050
and share some really
cool stories with you.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:30.883
Let's get started.

00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:33.400
I know what question
you want answered first.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.410
Is there so much loving
going on in the hospital

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:38.480
that we just can't control ourselves?

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.440
No, I have seen some residents hit it off,

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.906
I've seen some doctors and
nurses hit it off before,

00:00:43.906 --> 00:00:46.410
and start dating outside of the hospital,

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.960
so it does occur, but it
rarely, probably never,

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.510
occurs to the extent that people

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.880
are getting it on in janitorial closets.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.360
I will say this, I do
work with some nurses

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.640
who are in their 60s, and they've told me

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.740
that 20, 30 years ago,
it was more like that.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:06.651
They did go and visit some
doctors in the back rooms

00:01:06.651 --> 00:01:10.480
and in the boiler rooms,
and, maybe 20, 30 years ago,

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.520
before my time, it was more romantic

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:13.960
and similar to Grey's Anatomy.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.320
Sorry for those of you who wanted this,

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.410
but there's just not that much romance

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:20.403
that goes on in hospitals.

00:01:20.403 --> 00:01:23.100
(laughs)
- Excuse me?

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.220
- Next topic, money.
(cash register dings)

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.580
Everyone thinks that doctors are rich.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.960
The first thing I have to
say, right off the bat,

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.810
is med school is very expensive.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:36.450
Think Ferrari expensive, quarter
million dollars expensive.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.170
Then, the first three to five years

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.300
of being a doctor, out of medical school,

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.430
you're being paid a resident's salary,

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:45.040
which is very low.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.440
When I was training,
it ranged anywhere from

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.610
40 to 50,000 dollars a year.

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.940
Not a terrible salary, I agree with you,

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:56.800
but you're working sometimes
80 to 100 hours a week.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.740
If you calculate what
that comes out to hourly,

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.760
you're making around nine
to 10 dollars an hour.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.480
That's less than minimum
wage in some places.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.483
And then once you start a practice,

00:02:07.483 --> 00:02:10.550
you have to pay your
malpractice insurance,

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.190
which is sky-high for some specialties.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:16.380
To be practicing Ob-Gyn in New York City,

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.390
you're paying sometimes a hundred,

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.650
two hundred thousand dollars
a year alone for coverage.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:22.820
That's just coverage.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.620
Then if you want to open
up your own practice,

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:25.880
you have to pay for the space,

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.910
you wanna hire a nurse, and a PA

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:29.490
and a scribe and this and that,

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.020
you have to pay for that as well.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.180
But guess what, you still have

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:33.130
a quarter of a million dollars

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:35.190
in loans that you haven't
been able to pay back.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:38.540
So the whole notion that doctors
are rich right off the bat

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.277
and it's an easy career
that you can just go on

00:02:40.277 --> 00:02:43.000
and make a lot of money, false.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.540
Myth busted.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.290
My student loan debt, upon
graduating medical school

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:51.460
because I had some scholarships,
was about 220,000 or so.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.280
During my residency,
you would make payments,

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.030
and they wouldn't be large payments,

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:57.590
because they would be
based on your income.

00:02:57.590 --> 00:02:58.980
It's called income-based repayment,

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.060
but I noticed that after two
years of making these payments,

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.320
my amount that I owed actually went up

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:06.620
because of the percentage.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.400
- Just when I thought I was
out, they pull me back in!

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.980
- The next most common question probably

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:14.690
deals with losing a patient,

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:17.729
and being a resident, when you're on call,

00:03:17.729 --> 00:03:19.360
you're in the hospital at night

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:20.885
and you're basically responsible

00:03:20.885 --> 00:03:24.130
for any emergencies that go down.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.880
So if there's a code blue,
which means that someone's

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.390
heart has stopped, you're
the one that has to run there

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:33.120
to resuscitate them, to
bring them back to life.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.760
So to answer the question, absolutely,

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.940
I've lost patients on my watch.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:42.030
Not every resident or doctor
handles death the same way.

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:44.320
And we get a to of coaching and education

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:48.100
about what the right
way to handle death is,

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:51.390
and I don't; think
there's ever a right way,

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:52.870
but the one good point that's I've seen

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:55.430
at these coaching sessions
is that it's important

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:58.650
to not forcefully hide your emotions.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.600
And that's a natural
instinct that some people do,

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.290
they say, "I'm a doctor, I shouldn't cry."

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:05.680
Showing emotion is not a bad thing.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.570
It's that when you let
emotion take over you,

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:11.020
and it stops you from
making quality decisions,

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:13.460
that's where it sort of
crosses into territory

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:14.630
where it impedes your work.

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.910
It's very difficult to find the words

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:19.040
to share with the family members,

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:22.610
to help console them,
but you're the person.

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:24.000
You're the one that has to be there.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.800
I remember a specific story,
one night I was on call,

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:29.103
and there was a code blue in the ICU.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:32.340
There was a man, he was in his 90s,

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:35.490
and his heart stopped, we
tried to resuscitate him,

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.750
it was unsuccessful and
he ended up passing,

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:41.780
and his wife of 65 plus years was sitting

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:43.760
immediately outside of the room,

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:46.704
and I had to be the one
to break the news to her.

00:04:46.704 --> 00:04:50.960
So I got on my knee, I
sort of held her hand,

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:53.170
and I explained to her
everything, what happened,

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:55.500
and she was in shock, rightfully so.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:56.590
She'd just lost the partner

00:04:56.590 --> 00:05:00.410
she's been living with
for over half a century.

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:02.730
So I told her what
happened, I explained it,

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:05.200
she was very understanding and kind,

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:09.040
and I realized there wasn't anything more

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:12.040
I needed to say, but I couldn't leave,

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.540
I still needed to be
there, so I sat with her

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:17.660
for probably 20 minutes, on my knee,

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:19.180
just holding her hand,

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:21.330
and after 20 minutes of sitting there,

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:24.110
she looked at me and just said thank you.

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:25.990
And that's what she needed.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:28.750
Luckily for me that I didn't
have a page during that moment,

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:29.850
that I would have to leave.

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:32.860
You wanna give everyone
the proper amount of time

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:36.830
and have the conversation
to its full capacity, right,

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:38.840
to make sure the person is
okay with what happened,

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:40.930
they understand everything
that's going on.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:43.620
But remember, you're responsible
for the entire hospital.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:45.420
So your pager's constantly going off.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.050
There other medical emergencies
happening simultaneously.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:50.400
So not only do you have to be very kind

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:51.860
and understanding of what's going on,

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.100
you also have to be
realistic and explain to them

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:55.450
that you're gonna be back,

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:56.980
you're gonna have to deal
with another emergency,

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:58.830
because you are the
doctor that's responsible

00:05:58.830 --> 00:05:59.970
for the entire hospital.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:01.833
If you ever find yourself in a hospital,

00:06:01.833 --> 00:06:04.780
and you see that the
doctor is very stressed out

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:06.780
or feels like they're not just giving you

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:08.180
the proper amount of time,

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:10.280
ask if something urgent is going on

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:11.950
that they need to attend to first,

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:13.700
and so that they can come back maybe

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:15.770
and explain to you what's
going on a little bit,

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:17.400
because it's very rare that a doctor's

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:18.640
being rude just to be rude,

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:20.400
or a nurse is being rude just to be rude.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.830
Most of the time it's
'cause we're very busy

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:25.010
especially nowadays
with insurance companies

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:26.800
clamping down on profits,

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:29.670
the hospitals are often
slightly understaffed,

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:31.700
and we want to give you
the proper amount of time,

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.660
and oftentimes there's just so many things

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:35.550
going on that we oftentimes can't.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.740
As important as it is to talk about

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:39.501
moments of deaths in the hospital,

00:06:39.501 --> 00:06:42.360
there's also some really uplifting moments

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:43.400
when it comes to life.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.250
Being a family medicine physician,

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:48.577
I delivered just over 30 babies

00:06:48.577 --> 00:06:50.480
in Overlook Medical Center.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:53.640
One of the moms that I
had in the OB clinic,

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.460
she had a very difficult pregnancy,

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:59.370
she had a complication
regarding her gall bladder,

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:01.820
where she had to have
her gall bladder removed

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:02.890
while she was pregnant.

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:05.360
She had a very protracted
delivery, it was very long.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:10.360
I sat there probably with
her, you know, for 20 hours

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:12.710
and we were doing checks
every now and then

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:16.150
to make sure that her cervix
was still progressing,

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:19.420
and that we didn't need to
give her any medications,

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:20.253
and it was progressing,

00:07:20.253 --> 00:07:21.860
but it was just progressing very slowly,

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:24.946
and then, at midnight,
it's like the baby knew,

00:07:24.946 --> 00:07:28.070
it's midnight, Dr. Mike is exhausted,

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:30.030
it's time for me to poke my head out,

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:31.884
so we started the delivery,

00:07:31.884 --> 00:07:35.660
baby came out, perfect, crying right away,

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:37.400
I didn't have to spank the baby's bottom,

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:38.790
just kidding, you don't have to do that.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:40.610
Instantly take the baby and put the baby

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:43.100
on the mom;'s chest so they
can have some interaction.

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:45.552
Looking at the mom's
face immediately after

00:07:45.552 --> 00:07:49.160
the delivery is such an amazing moment,

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:52.890
because it's a complex
emotion that she's showing.

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:55.350
She's anxious, wants reassurance

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:56.210
that they baby's okay,

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:58.010
she's happy, she's exited.

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:00.130
There's just so much emotion that comes

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:03.340
out of that one first smile
when the baby's first born.

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:04.650
There's really nothing else like it.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.960
Finally, let's talk about the inaccuracies

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.980
that come with some of these TV shows.

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:11.980
As a doctor, it's painful
to watch them at times,

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:15.260
because there's a lot of
medical errors that are made.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:17.670
They shock asystole with the paddles,

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:18.940
and not everybody understands that,

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.890
but as a doctor it's so frustrating

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:23.200
to see them give bad medical care.

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:25.630
In the TV dramas, doctors do everything.

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:27.180
They meet the patient in the ER,

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:28.480
they take them for the scans,

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:31.270
they perform the scans,
they read the scans.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:33.030
There's a lot of nursing
care involved there

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:34.570
and it's a shame that these medical dramas

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:35.740
leaves the nurses out.

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:37.520
The nurse will not only take your vitals,

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:38.910
they'll give you their medications

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:42.090
pretty much the only interaction
you have with the doctor

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:44.060
is describing what's going on,

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:46.250
hearing what the doctor's
point of view is,

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:47.390
and that's pretty much it.

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:49.525
The rest of the time
you're being surrounded by

00:08:49.525 --> 00:08:52.450
tech staff, MAs, nursing staff,

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.150
and those are the people who are really

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:56.860
the backbone of medicine that I applaud,

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:58.760
and I feel like don't
get recognized enough

00:08:58.760 --> 00:08:59.720
in TV dramas.

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:01.720
I hope you enjoyed hearing
about my experiences

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:03.170
in the hospital system.

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:04.890
It's definitely an interesting place,

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:06.940
as you can tell, I'm
very passionate about it,

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:07.900
so if you want to learn more

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:09.570
and you have some questions or comments,

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:10.640
leave them down below.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:12.260
As you know, that I'm very active

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:13.300
in the comments section.

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:15.320
I have a fun proposition for you.

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:17.650
If you get this video to 10,000 likes,

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:19.680
which is very doable, you've done that

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.100
for a lot of my videos already.

